# Navigation

Portable navigation v2 sources use `golem/geometry` binary sidecars for canonical meshes and compact float32 terrain samples/holes. `navbuild.DecodeFile` and `DecodeWithGeometry` verify contained paths, exact hashes and expansion bounds, then use `Scene.Validate` without retaining expanded placements or computing unused fingerprints; `BakeFile` uses that path. Preserve strict/non-strict geometry admission, legacy inline sources and navigation's b-c terrain diagonal, independently of physics's a-d split. Instance transforms and area semantics remain explicit; this does not change streaming or bake coverage policy.

Idle staged-physics attachments must refresh regional quarantine after geometry changes even without a destination; stopping navigation for a scripted physical trajectory must not freeze that body forever after safety returns. Timed physical actions use captured navigation safety and actual-path reconciliation while their own intent owns motion. Legacy synchronous idle scheduling remains unchanged.

Activation-first physical edits retain full regional quarantine between tick-side geometry activation and asynchronous navigation publication. Failed builds leave the actual physical state explicit and pending. Restart captures accepted navigation separately from desired navigation; a pending checkpoint requires private matching physics/navigation reconstruction before either becomes ready. Invalidate all worker generations prepared against the replaced world, even completed results waiting to drain.

Paired physical restoration and prepared geometry publication live in root `golem/physics_checkpoint.go` and `golem/physics_geometry.go`, using the existing navigation restoration barrier and bounded stable-key build queue. Keep immutable physics/navigation identity captures paired. Establish full regional quarantine before physical changes, preserve unaffected agents, and open restoration readiness only after entity/body/support intent publication. Source lease handles are rebuilt from stable IDs; routes and query handles are never restored. Before a staged frame seals, discard superseded plans and recapture all safety after callbacks without rerunning the scheduler.

`golem/nav`, `nav/pathing`, and `nav/kelindar` are separate Go modules developed through the ignored local `go.work`. The shared nav module does not depend on the root runtime, map loaders, or either pathfinding library.

Root `golem/navigation_frame.go` coordinates optional staged motion using the existing surface contracts; no physics dependency enters shared nav. Prepare scheduling happens once per tick, with ordered segments apportioned to the physical frame duration. Generation, snapshot, source position and regional safety stay captured until full-path validation. Every physically affected attachment, including idle agents, reconciles actual float32 anchor positions; unsupported physical results are not snapped to another surface. Quarantine requires a solver-side hold before movement, while unexpected post-solve violations reject the entire interacting frame. Transactional traversal actions commit after all navigation state is reconciled. The synchronous navigation/motor API remains separate and compatible.

Fixed native physics stepping preserves this host safety boundary. Validate captured navigation segments and curved-anchor bounds at every observed native step; extra retained corner/support intervals share one frame duration and cannot rerun fairness or multiply force integration. Whole-frame navigation rejection is not a universal physical penetration ceiling. Solver contact compliance does not relax query/placement precision or permit individual-body rollback after interactions.

`nav/surface` belongs to the shared nav module and defines Y-up, float64 world-space contracts with opaque snapshot-qualified locations and immutable route views. Query scratch is exclusively owned. `View.Release` is idempotent; handles cannot outlive their view. `CorridorQuery` repairs paths; optional `RegionalBackend` supplies coherent immutable safety captures with committed/desired revisions and change tokens. Backend-owned location, corridor, segment and bounds checks hide tile identities from the runtime. `RebaseQuery` transfers unchanged polygon support and corridors to a retained latest view; it must not choose another floor. `CornerAppender` uses caller-owned capacity without borrowing query scratch. Optional `SupportIdentity` identifies the same live convex support polygon within one retained view; it is not a proximity or reachability test.

Optional `CorridorTransferQuery` transfers the entire unchanged corridor without repair, trimming or corner recomputation. Preserve geometry, corner order, endpoints and limiting status; the caller preserves its cursor and any outstanding deviation requiring repair. Reject changed dependencies instead of returning a repaired prefix. `RebaseQuery` may repair and trim a corridor, including a Partial result. Both transfer exact support only, allow a nil route for support-only transfer, and require the old view to stay retained until replacement handles are accepted together.

`nav/navmesh` is a separate module depending on navmesh-go and shared nav. Static snapshots and dynamic managed leases implement the same surface contract. It owns no root runtime/entity imports, worker scheduling or gameplay actions. Released/foreign handles return Stale. Preserve semantic result codes and partial-result reasons rather than flattening them to booleans. Dynamic safety quarantines dirty tile columns across every vertical layer and bake/clearance influence. Safety is cached by change token; failed/cancelled builds do not clear it. `PendingBackend` alone retains conservative profile-wide behavior. Movement uses the library operation without visited output; returned routes and corner/link values remain independently owned.

Optional `RetainedRegionalSafety` protects old-view movement with the union of changed columns since every still-leased retired generation. Publication retains those columns until all relevant old leases are released; the last release changes the safety token. Captures remain immutable, and releasing one generation must not erase changes still needed by an older generation or pending desired edits. `HasQuarantine` can be true with equal committed/desired revisions. Keep this protection backend-owned; ordinary segment checks alone cannot justify deferred rebasing.

Optional `ViewRegionalSafety.ForView` selects a cached generation-specific capture: pending/unloaded columns plus changes since that view, with full segment/bounds protection. Reject foreign, released and unrecognized views and mismatched handle generations. Scope selection must not rebuild dirty lists per agent or retain runtime snapshots. Current views need not wait for unrelated old leases; the global capture remains conservative for consumers without scoped selection.

Scoped library safety recognizes exact captured snapshot identities, not equal world/profile/revision metadata. A no-op publication can create another snapshot at the same revision; keep the bounded set of actual current/leased identities for that scope. Its complete changed-column history permits local ownership checks instead of repeated latest-topology comparisons. Last noncurrent lease release invalidates scope membership/tokens even if no quarantine columns were added; prior captures remain immutable. Global captures keep latest-dependency validation.

Cached topology/safety identities are lightweight immutable metadata, not snapshot ownership. Exact private snapshot identity can skip redundant dependency checks; equal public world/revision values cannot. Cross-snapshot use must still validate settings/profile compatibility and tile revisions. Sharing private immutable path arrays during strict transfer must not retain the old runtime snapshot or expose mutable storage through public copied results.

Dynamic column rebuilds may share private immutable construction arrays, never runtime tile bytes or adjacency. Copy caller-owned replacements, replace all layers of selected columns, check compatible world/profile/settings, and validate the full merged artifact under normal portable resource limits. New snapshots must not retain base snapshot pointers. Public bundle exports remain independent copies.

Authored-link metadata changes dirty endpoint/radius influence columns across the finite region height. Link attachment can succeed outside authored source Y bounds within climb tolerance; vertical AABB rejection must never hide its metadata change. Preserve old/new XZ influence and finite bounds, then retain the normal bake/clearance padding.

`nav/navmesh/navbuild` and its `cmd/golem-nav-bake` consume explicit `{Generator?, Scene, Config}` JSON matching navmesh-go's generic source contract. Input is bounded and unknown fields rejected. Geometry generation lives in navmesh-go; collision footprints remain collision-only. `golem-bake` retains schema/code-generation responsibilities. Scribe may export explicit server navigation source but does not bake with Unity navigation.

Root `golem/navigation*.go` owns queues, lifecycle, movement, motor/traversal callbacks, readiness and stable intent checkpoints. The adapter module must not import it. `examples/navigation-server` is an independent nested module proving bake/artifact/load/update/restart through the server.

`examples/navigation-stress` is a separate qualification consumer of the actual server; seeded geometry comes from navmesh-go's `qualification/worldgen`. `qualification/` stages exact working sources and private candidate module archives, without editing tracked module files. Build/run material, source copies, caches and evidence belong under `C:/Demiurgos/navmesh-validation/world-test`. Keep generated worlds out of Git and required configuration fixtures in ordinary Git. Record failures and missing measurements; timing gates cannot be passed by reducing movement cadence. Timed workloads run without simultaneous fuzzing or unrelated benchmarks.

Mixed-scene mode counts do not prove physical motion. Record final reconciled displacement against the same tick's starting body position, with an explicit tolerance and separate host ejection/fall counts; exclude ticks crossing measurement boundaries. The opt-in static `Capacity` workload preserves normal speed and uses long startup routes without destination or geometry churn. Verify both profile directions with the same bounded query limits used by the server, require startup admission before measurement and every agent moving on each complete measured tick. Report startup burst latency separately from the mixed-workload request gate. The test motor resolves static exclusion walls only, without agent-agent collision or avoidance. `Suite` runs terrain, multilevel and construction at 1,000 agents each; `Full` adds the existing two-hour 1,000-agent construction soak. The 100-agent and 5,000-agent workloads are outside the user-selected qualification scope. Existing runner/rehearsal/soak count controls remain available for explicit diagnostics. Capacity is a separate 1,000-agent run.

Capacity preflight also verifies continuous physical cylinder clearance against the unchanged source exclusion boxes. Keep physical `BodyProfiles` distinct from baked profiles, match them by ID, and record the explicit fixture bake-radius margin. Legacy fixtures may use baked dimensions for bodies only when optional body metadata is absent and the margin is zero. Do not enlarge physical motors when introducing conservative bake clearance. Preserve unsafe legacy-fixture preflight coverage and exact offending segment/obstacle/separation evidence; the capacity-specific margin does not change mixed-family bake settings or establish a general clearance guarantee.

Queue limits include retained build-key status (`BuildHistoryCapacity`), with explicit forgetting only after cancellation/completion and globally unique generations across key reuse. Delayed, queued and active build keys share a bounded capacity; debounce defaults to 50 ms and caps eligibility delay at 250 ms. Only explicitly retryable transient errors get bounded backoff; the adapter marks `dynamic.ErrBusy`, never cancellation or stale candidates. Keep tick-owned delayed requests separate from worker-owned scratch, and expose their wait through status timestamps and delayed counts. Callbacks can change intent or detach/re-attach entities; fence by attachment/generation before applying their results. Checkpoint validation bounds the entire base64 JSON envelope, not only desired-world payload bytes.

Managed agents reject partial paths unless `AllowPartial` is enabled, retaining copied diagnostic corners for rejected prefixes. Active path work has a deadline; late success cannot override cancellation. Traversal timeout uses processed movement time, and stalled replanning has a finite attempt limit. Preserve these policy limits when changing movement/corridor code.

`nav/internal/mapinput.RowMajor` borrows raw Tiled/LDtk values during synchronous construction. It owns row-major indexing and the rule that zero is blocked without calling the blocked callback. Nonzero values call the supplied predicate. Constructors keep validation order, origins, dimensions, library allocation, and bool-versus-cost adaptation. Do not silently introduce nil-callback defaults, data-length validation, or GID decoding.

Keep backend-specific coordinate rounding, numeric limits, weighted costs, and reconstruction separate. Pathing uses truncated integer cell dimensions/half-centers and chained partial paths. Kelindar uses floating dimensions, int16 coordinates, padded pages, and weighted reconstruction. Same-cell requests currently return the exact start before checking walkability. The backend tests characterize these behaviors; they are not instructions to fix them as part of refactoring.

Known deferred corrections include kelindar page-rounding overflow at 32767 and raw-cost narrowing, endpoint walkability versus the interface promise, and pathing's shared AStar scratch versus its concurrent-use comment. Do not infer thread safety from that comment or merge endpoint/segment policies to hide these differences.

Release nav with mapinput before raising backend dependency pins. Verify each nested module explicitly and standalone against intended dependency versions; do not commit workspace files or local replace directives.

Navigation release order is shared nav v0.4.0 and navmesh-go v0.1.0, adapter nav/navmesh v0.1.0, then root consumer release. These intended versions must exist before standalone release qualification. Temporary external modfiles can test unreleased source; never claim those checks validate published resolution, and never publish without the user's publication authorization.

After each dependency is actually published, run `GOWORK=off go mod tidy` in its consumer modules, record real proxy checksums, and repeat ordinary test/vet checks. Candidate file-proxy archive checksums belong only in temporary test copies and must not be committed as release sums. The navigation CI lane intentionally depends on this release preparation.

The pure-Go and server CI matrices include native Linux ARM64 (`ubuntu-24.04-arm`) execution. An ARM64 cross-build alone is not execution qualification; keep the hosted runner gate explicit until that lane has actually passed.

For the physics workstream, the user's 2026-11-12 scope decision selects Windows AMD64 and native Linux AMD64 only after the retained navigation manifests confirmed ARM64 compilation but no native ARM64 execution. Historical navigation evidence and CI configuration stay unchanged; an unexecuted CI entry does not establish a reusable native host.
